WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.267
Git keeps your project in three places.

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.627
The working tree is the files in your project folder, the ones your editor changes.

3
00:00:10.833 --> 00:00:16.344
The staging area, also called the index, holds the exact contents of your next commit.

4
00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:20.883
The repository is the hidden .git folder, where every commit lives.

5
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.860
Say you change the price file in your editor.

6
00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:25.700
Only the working tree changes.

7
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.843
git add copies that change into the staging area.

8
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:35.236
git commit turns the whole staging area into a new commit in the repository.

9
00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:40.229
Most people picture a commit as a diff: the lines you changed.

10
00:00:40.229 --> 00:00:42.211
Git does not store it that way.

11
00:00:43.633 --> 00:00:47.479
Each commit records the whole project, as it was at that moment.

12
00:00:47.479 --> 00:00:50.303
Git works out a diff only when you ask for one.

13
00:00:50.533 --> 00:00:52.826
Git keeps three kinds of objects.

14
00:00:52.826 --> 00:00:55.466
A blob holds the contents of one file.

15
00:00:55.466 --> 00:00:58.105
Only the bytes: no file name, no date.

16
00:00:58.333 --> 00:00:59.752
A tree is one folder.

17
00:00:59.752 --> 00:01:04.277
It lists names, and each name points to a blob, or to another tree.

18
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.233
A commit points to one tree, the top folder of the project.

19
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:12.663
It also holds its parent commit, the author, the time and the message.

20
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:15.853
Every object is named by a hash of its content.

21
00:01:15.853 --> 00:01:19.792
A hash turns any bytes into a short, fixed-length fingerprint.

22
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.385
Git uses SHA-1, which gives forty hex characters, and it usually shows only the first seven.

23
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:30.071
The same content gets the same name on every computer.

24
00:01:30.071 --> 00:01:34.763
Hash the word hello on your machine or on mine, and you get exactly this.

25
00:01:35.567 --> 00:01:39.515
So a file that did not change is the same blob in every commit.

26
00:01:39.515 --> 00:01:42.023
Here is the commit just before this one.

27
00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:45.129
Both trees point to the same README blob.

28
00:01:45.129 --> 00:01:46.471
Git stores it once.

29
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.165
The price file did change, so each commit points to its own blob for it.

30
00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:55.950
A commit's hash covers its tree, its parent, its author line and its message.

31
00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:01.129
Change even one letter of the message, and you get a different commit, with a new hash.

32
00:02:01.333 --> 00:02:02.969
Remember that for Day 9.

33
00:02:02.969 --> 00:02:06.104
It is why a rebase gives your commits new IDs.

34
00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:10.623
Commits point back to their parents, so history is a chain.

35
00:02:10.623 --> 00:02:13.361
You can follow it back, one parent at a time.

36
00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:18.819
A branch is a small file that holds one commit hash.

37
00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:21.383
main points to the newest commit on main.

38
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.449
And currency-first is another branch, pointing at the commit on its side branch.

39
00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:29.066
HEAD answers the question: where am I?

40
00:02:29.066 --> 00:02:32.035
Usually it points to a branch, not to a commit.

41
00:02:32.267 --> 00:02:33.454
Now make a commit.

42
00:02:33.454 --> 00:02:38.467
Git writes a new commit object, and its parent is the commit main points to.

43
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.483
Then it moves main forward to the new commit.

44
00:02:41.483 --> 00:02:44.203
HEAD comes along, because it points at main.

45
00:02:44.203 --> 00:02:45.440
No files get copied.

46
00:02:45.667 --> 00:02:49.757
So git commit moves one pointer: the branch that HEAD points to.

47
00:02:49.757 --> 00:02:51.995
currency-first did not move at all.

48
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.584
Most undo commands work the same way.

49
00:02:54.584 --> 00:03:00.512
A hard reset two commits back moves main to an older commit, and resets your files to match.

50
00:03:00.733 --> 00:03:02.753
The newer commits are not deleted.

51
00:03:02.753 --> 00:03:06.613
They are still in .git, and the reflog can lead you back to them.

52
00:03:06.833 --> 00:03:10.605
A commit is a full snapshot, named by a hash of its content.

53
00:03:10.605 --> 00:03:15.572
Branches and HEAD are pointers, and git commit moves the branch HEAD points to.
